<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dominica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Commonwealth of Dominica, Wai'tu kubuli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dominica is an island country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-islands">Windward Islands</a> in the Lesser Antilles, between the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Guadeloupe and Martinique. Arawak settlers arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> in the fifth century and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> displaced them by the 15th century, calling the island Wai'tu kubuli. The French held it from 1690 to 1763 and enslaved West Africans to work <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> took possession in 1763 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> and continued forced labor, gradually making English the official language. Dominica was governed as part of the British Windward Islands until 1958, and the island gained independence as a republic in 1978.</p></div>
